## Why this theorem is needed

G3CE performs exact block row elimination on the terminal affine connection
matrix. That preserves its static rank, kernel, cokernel and selected-source
obstruction. It does not automatically preserve the differential equation

```text
dz/da=K_C(a)z+b_C(a).
```

A time-dependent connection changes under a differentiable gauge, not under
bare row elimination. G3CG makes that boundary exact.

## Terminal static graph

At the G3CE good fiber, put

```text
X=J^-1L,
y=J^-1b_lower,
z_lower=-X z_H20-y.
```

The static lower equation vanishes on this graph, and the upper equation is

```text
z_H20'=S_C z_H20+b_eff.
```

For the frozen graph to be dynamically invariant, its lower derivative would
also have to equal `-X(S_C z_H20+b_eff)`. Exact computation gives

```text
rank(X)                  = 248,
rank(X S_C)              = 247,
rank([X S_C | X b_eff])  = 248,
nnz(X b_eff)             = 1,132.
```

The static graph is therefore not invariant. The selected source adds one
independent affine defect direction beyond the homogeneous rank-247 defect.
Simple lower-coordinate back-substitution cannot reduce transport.

## Dynamic Riccati cutset

Let the same graph vary rationally with `a`. Under the explicit G3CD plus-sign
convention, differentiating

```text
z_lower=-X(a)z_H20-y(a)
```

shows that it is invariant only if

```text
X'(a)+X(a)S_C(a)=0,
y'(a)+X(a)b_eff(a)=0.
```

At `a=0`, G3CG emits the exact necessary derivative targets

```text
X'(0)=-X S_C,
y'(0)=-X b_eff
```

over `GF(11)`. The first target has rank 247; the second has 1,132 nonzero
entries. Computing the actual family derivatives and finding one mismatch is
a rigorous no-go for this particular 249-variable transport reduction.
Agreement at the endpoint is necessary but not sufficient: the identities
must then be certified along the selected path.

## Claim boundary

G3CG does not compute the actual derivatives of the rational family, prove or
exclude every possible dynamic gauge, or integrate the selected path. Until a
Riccati/invariant-graph theorem passes, the affine Gauss-Manin transport remains
1,510-dimensional. G3CE and G3CF remain useful exact static obstruction and
domain theorems.

